; ================================================================
;  3D Graphics -- new nodes for sb.guide
;  Append this file to the end of sb.guide, then apply the
;  two edits described in sb_3d_patch_instructions.md.
; ================================================================


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  Sec_3D -- Section overview (linked from MAIN)
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE Sec_3D "3D Graphics"
@{b}3D Graphics@{ub}

SpecBAS provides a complete software 3D engine with perspective
projection, Gouraud shading, texturing, fog, and multi-threaded
rendering. Models are built from vertices and faces, placed as
instances in a scene, and rendered into any window or graphic bank.

@{CODE}
@{"  3D Concepts and Coordinates      " LINK 3D_CONCEPTS}
@{"  SCENE - Scene Management         " LINK SCENE}
@{"  MODEL NEW - Creating Models      " LINK MODEL_NEW}
@{"  MODEL VERTEX/FACE/BUILD          " LINK MODEL_VERTEX}
@{"  MODEL AT/MOVE/ROTATE/SCALE       " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"  MODEL SHADING/INK/BILLBOARD      " LINK MODEL_SHADING}
@{"  MODEL Animation and Parenting    " LINK MODEL_ANIM}
@{"  CAMERA                           " LINK CAMERA}
@{"  RENDER                           " LINK RENDER}
@{"  LIGHT, FOG and SETNEAR           " LINK LIGHT}
@{"  Model Instance Functions         " LINK MODEL_FUNCS}
@{"  Model State and Picking          " LINK MODEL_INFO}
@{"  Camera Functions                 " LINK CAMERA_FUNCS}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  3D_CONCEPTS -- Coordinate system and core concepts
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE 3D_CONCEPTS "3D Graphics - Concepts"
@{b}3D Concepts and Coordinate System@{ub}

SpecBAS uses a right-handed coordinate system. Looking along the
negative Z axis (into the screen):

@{CODE}
  Y up
  |
  |
  +----X right
 /
Z toward viewer
@{BODY}

@{b}X@{ub} increases to the right. @{b}Y@{ub} increases upward. @{b}Z@{ub} increases toward
the viewer, so objects further away have smaller (more negative) Z.

Rotations are in degrees about each axis. @{b}RX@{ub} tilts forward and back
(pitch). @{b}RY@{ub} turns left and right (yaw). @{b}RZ@{ub} rolls around the
viewing axis.

@{b}Models and Instances@{ub}

A @{b}model@{ub} is a reusable mesh stored in a bank. It defines the geometry
-- vertex positions, faces, normals, UV coordinates and shading flags
-- but has no position of its own. A model is placed into the world
by creating an @{b}instance@{ub} with @{b}MODEL AT@{ub}. The same model can have
many instances simultaneously, each at a different position,
rotation and scale, at very low memory cost.

@{b}Scenes@{ub}

A @{b}scene@{ub} is a bank that holds a list of instances. All RENDER and
instance-movement commands operate on whichever scene is active at
the time. A program can maintain multiple scenes and switch between
them with @{b}SCENE USE@{ub}; each scene stores its own camera independently.

@{b}Workflow@{ub}

@{CODE}
  1. SCENE NEW s           -- create a scene bank
  2. MODEL NEW m           -- create a model bank
  3. MODEL VERTEX m, ...   -- add vertices
  4. MODEL FACE m, ...     -- add faces
  5. MODEL BUILD m         -- compile geometry
  6. MODEL AT i, m, ...    -- place an instance
  7. CAMERA x,y,z,rx,ry,rz -- position the camera
  8. LIGHT TO dx,dy,dz     -- optional: set light direction
  9. RENDER s              -- draw the scene
@{BODY}

Steps 3-5 are done once when setting up a model. Steps 6-9 are
part of the main loop.

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"SCENE         " LINK SCENE}
@{"MODEL NEW     " LINK MODEL_NEW}
@{"MODEL VERTEX  " LINK MODEL_VERTEX}
@{"MODEL AT      " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"CAMERA        " LINK CAMERA}
@{"RENDER        " LINK RENDER}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  SCENE -- Scene management
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE SCENE "SCENE"
@{b}SCENE NEW/USE/CLEAR/ERASE@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}SCENE NEW numvar@{fg text}
@{fg 1}SCENE USE id@{fg text}
@{fg 1}SCENE CLEAR [id]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}SCENE ERASE id@{fg text}
@{BODY}

@{b}SCENE NEW@{ub} creates a new, empty scene bank and assigns its bank ID
to @{b}numvar@{ub}. The new scene becomes the active scene automatically if
no other scene is currently active. A scene stores a list of model
instances and its own camera position.

@{b}SCENE USE@{ub} makes an existing scene the active scene. All subsequent
@{b}MODEL AT@{ub}, @{b}MODEL MOVE@{ub}, @{b}CAMERA@{ub}, @{b}RENDER@{ub} and related commands
operate on the active scene. Switching scenes with @{b}SCENE USE@{ub}
automatically saves the current camera into the outgoing scene and
restores the camera from the incoming one.

@{b}SCENE CLEAR@{ub} removes all instances from a scene but leaves the scene
bank itself intact, ready to be populated again. If @{b}id@{ub} is omitted,
the active scene is cleared. The camera is not affected.

@{b}SCENE ERASE@{ub} deletes the scene bank entirely. If the erased scene was
active, no scene remains active until @{b}SCENE USE@{ub} or @{b}SCENE NEW@{ub} is
called. All instances in the scene are removed but the model banks
they referenced are not deleted.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Two independent scenes, each with its own camera
20 SCENE NEW sc1
30 SCENE NEW sc2
40 REM  Build sc1 with the camera close up
50 SCENE USE sc1
60 CAMERA 0,0,5, 0,0,0
70 REM  ... add instances to sc1 ...
80 REM  Build sc2 with a wider overview shot
90 SCENE USE sc2
100 CAMERA 0,10,20, -20,0,0
110 REM  ... add instances to sc2 ...
120 DO
130   SCENE USE sc1 : RENDER sc1
140   PAUSE 100
150   SCENE USE sc2 : RENDER sc2
160   PAUSE 100
170 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"MODEL NEW     " LINK MODEL_NEW}
@{"MODEL AT      " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"CAMERA        " LINK CAMERA}
@{"RENDER        " LINK RENDER}
@{"BANK          " LINK BANK}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  MODEL_NEW -- What a model is; MODEL NEW
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE MODEL_NEW "MODEL NEW"
@{b}MODEL NEW@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}MODEL NEW numvar@{fg text}
@{BODY}

Creates a new, empty model bank and assigns its ID to @{b}numvar@{ub}. The
model initially has no vertices or faces. You build it by calling
@{b}MODEL VERTEX@{ub} and @{b}MODEL FACE@{ub} as many times as needed, then finish
with @{b}MODEL BUILD@{ub} to compile the geometry.

A model bank is a reusable mesh. It has no position in the world --
to display it, place one or more instances with @{b}MODEL AT@{ub}. Many
instances of the same model can exist simultaneously at different
positions, scales and rotations, sharing a single copy of the mesh
data.

Model banks can be saved and loaded with @{b}SAVE BANK@{ub} and @{b}LOAD BANK@{ub}
in the normal way.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 SCENE NEW sc
20 MODEL NEW m
30 REM  -- add vertices and faces here --
40 MODEL BUILD m
50 MODEL AT i, m, 0,0,-5, 0,0,0
60 CAMERA 0,0,0, 0,0,0
70 RENDER sc
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"MODEL VERTEX  " LINK MODEL_VERTEX}
@{"MODEL AT      " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"SCENE NEW     " LINK SCENE}
@{"BANK          " LINK BANK}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  MODEL_VERTEX -- Geometry building
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE MODEL_VERTEX "MODEL VERTEX"
@{b}MODEL VERTEX / MODEL FACE / MODEL BUILD / MODEL SETVERT / MODEL UV@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}MODEL VERTEX model, x, y, z [, colour]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL VERTEX model, array()@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL FACE model, v0, v1, v2 [, v3 ...] [INK colour]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL FACE model, v0, v1, v2 [, v3 ...] [GRAPHIC id, u0,v0, u1,v1, u2,v2 [,...]]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL FACE model, array()@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL BUILD model@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL SETVERT model, index, x, y, z@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL UV model, face AT u0,v0, u1,v1, u2,v2@{fg text}
@{BODY}

@{b}MODEL VERTEX@{ub} adds one vertex to the model at position @{b}x@{ub},@{b}y@{ub},@{b}z@{ub}.
The optional @{b}colour@{ub} is a palette index used for Gouraud-shaded
rendering; if omitted it defaults to the current ink colour. The
return value (pushed onto the stack) is the zero-based index of
the new vertex, which is used in @{b}MODEL FACE@{ub} to reference it.
Vertices are numbered from zero in the order they are added.

The array form @{b}MODEL VERTEX model, array()@{ub} bulk-loads vertices from
a numeric array. The array must have a second dimension of at least
3 (x,y,z per row); a fourth element per row is treated as the
colour index. This is the fastest way to load a large mesh.

@{b}MODEL FACE@{ub} adds a polygon to the model. @{b}v0@{ub}, @{b}v1@{ub}, @{b}v2@{ub} (and any
further @{b}vN@{ub}) are vertex indices from previous @{b}MODEL VERTEX@{ub} calls.
At least three vertices are required. Faces with more than three
vertices are automatically triangulated as a fan from @{b}v0@{ub}.

The winding order determines which side of the face is the front
(visible) side. Looking at the front face, vertices should be
listed in anti-clockwise order.

The optional @{b}INK colour@{ub} sets a per-face flat colour overriding any
vertex colours. Without @{b}INK@{ub} or @{b}GRAPHIC@{ub}, the face uses vertex
colours (Gouraud shading is enabled separately with @{b}MODEL SHADING@{ub}).

The optional @{b}GRAPHIC id@{ub} clause makes the face textured. @{b}id@{ub} is the
bank ID of an 8bpp graphic bank used as the texture. Following
@{b}GRAPHIC id@{ub} you must supply one pair of UV coordinates (0.0 to 1.0)
for each vertex: @{b}u0,v0, u1,v1, u2,v2@{ub} (and further pairs for
polygons with more than three vertices). U increases left to right
and V increases top to bottom in the texture image.

The array form @{b}MODEL FACE model, array()@{ub} bulk-loads face definitions.
Each row must contain exactly three vertex indices (triangle only).
No colour or texture information is included; faces take vertex
colours. The array form is fastest for importing large meshes.

@{b}MODEL BUILD@{ub} compiles the model after all vertices and faces have
been added. It computes face normals for lighting, builds the edge
list for wireframe rendering, and calculates the bounding sphere
used for frustum culling and collision detection. @{b}MODEL BUILD@{ub} must
be called before the first @{b}RENDER@{ub}. If you modify vertices later
with @{b}MODEL SETVERT@{ub}, call @{b}MODEL BUILD@{ub} again to update the normals
and bounding sphere.

@{b}MODEL SETVERT@{ub} changes the position of an existing vertex by its
zero-based @{b}index@{ub}. Useful for morph animation without a frame system.
Call @{b}MODEL BUILD@{ub} after modifying vertices to keep normals correct.

@{b}MODEL UV@{ub} updates the texture coordinates of a specific face after
the model has been built. @{b}face@{ub} is the zero-based face index.
The @{b}AT@{ub} keyword is required. Coordinates are in the range 0.0 to 1.0.
This allows scrolling or swapping UV regions at runtime without
rebuilding the entire model.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Build a coloured triangle
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 MODEL NEW m
40 MODEL VERTEX m, -1,-1, 0, 2    : REM v0 red
50 MODEL VERTEX m,  1,-1, 0, 6    : REM v1 yellow
60 MODEL VERTEX m,  0, 1, 0, 1    : REM v2 blue
70 MODEL FACE m, 0,1,2
80 MODEL BUILD m
90 MODEL SHADING m SMOOTH
100 MODEL AT i, m, 0,0,-5, 0,0,0
110 CAMERA 0,0,0, 0,0,0
120 DO
130   CLS
140   MODEL ROTATE i, 0,1,0
150   RENDER sc
160   WAIT SCREEN
170 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Textured quad (two triangles sharing a graphic)
20 GRAPHIC NEW g, 0,0, 64,64      : REM load or draw texture here
30 SCENE NEW sc
40 MODEL NEW m
50 MODEL VERTEX m, -1,-1, 0
60 MODEL VERTEX m,  1,-1, 0
70 MODEL VERTEX m,  1, 1, 0
80 MODEL VERTEX m, -1, 1, 0
90 MODEL FACE m, 0,1,2 GRAPHIC g, 0,1, 1,1, 1,0
100 MODEL FACE m, 0,2,3 GRAPHIC g, 0,1, 1,0, 0,0
110 MODEL BUILD m
120 MODEL AT i, m, 0,0,-3, 0,0,0
130 CAMERA 0,0,0, 0,0,0
140 RENDER sc
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Bulk-load a mesh from arrays
20 DIM v(8,3)                      : REM 8 vertices, x y z
30 REM  Unit cube vertices
40 FOR n=0 TO 7
50   v(n,0) = (n AND 1)*2-1
60   v(n,1) = ((n SHR 1) AND 1)*2-1
70   v(n,2) = ((n SHR 2) AND 1)*2-1
80 NEXT n
90 DIM f(12,3)                     : REM 12 triangles
100 REM  Six faces of the cube, two triangles each
110 REM  ... fill f() with vertex indices ...
120 MODEL NEW m
130 MODEL VERTEX m, v()
140 MODEL FACE m, f()
150 MODEL BUILD m
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"MODEL NEW     " LINK MODEL_NEW}
@{"MODEL AT      " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"MODEL SHADING " LINK MODEL_SHADING}
@{"GRAPHIC       " LINK GRAPHIC}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  MODEL_AT -- Placing and moving instances
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE MODEL_AT "MODEL AT"
@{b}MODEL AT / MODEL MOVE / MODEL ROTATE / MODEL SCALE / MODEL HIDE / MODEL SHOW / MODEL ERASE@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}MODEL AT numvar, model, x, y, z, rx, ry, rz [, scale] [BILLBOARD] [PARENT id]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL MOVE inst, dx, dy, dz@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL MOVE inst, TO x, y, z@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL ROTATE inst, drx, dry, drz@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL ROTATE inst, TO rx, ry, rz@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL SCALE inst, s@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL HIDE inst@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL SHOW inst@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL ERASE inst@{fg text}
@{BODY}

@{b}MODEL AT@{ub} places a new instance of @{b}model@{ub} into the active scene and
assigns its instance ID to @{b}numvar@{ub}. @{b}x@{ub},@{b}y@{ub},@{b}z@{ub} set the world position.
@{b}rx@{ub},@{b}ry@{ub},@{b}rz@{ub} set the rotation angles in degrees. The optional @{b}scale@{ub}
multiplies the model's size uniformly; default is 1.0.

The optional @{b}BILLBOARD@{ub} flag makes the instance always face the
camera regardless of its @{b}ry@{ub} rotation, which is useful for
sprites, trees, and flat decals in a 3D world.

The optional @{b}PARENT id@{ub} attaches this instance to a parent instance
so that when the parent moves or rotates, this instance moves with
it. See @{b}MODEL PARENT@{ub} in @{"MODEL ANIM" LINK MODEL_ANIM} for further detail.

@{b}MODEL MOVE@{ub} adds @{b}dx@{ub},@{b}dy@{ub},@{b}dz@{ub} to the instance's current position
(relative move). The @{b}TO@{ub} form sets the position to an absolute
value @{b}x@{ub},@{b}y@{ub},@{b}z@{ub} directly.

@{b}MODEL ROTATE@{ub} adds @{b}drx@{ub},@{b}dry@{ub},@{b}drz@{ub} to the current rotation angles
(relative rotate). The @{b}TO@{ub} form sets the rotation to an absolute
value.

@{b}MODEL SCALE@{ub} sets the uniform scale factor of an instance to @{b}s@{ub}.
A value of 2 doubles the size; 0.5 halves it. Unlike MOVE and
ROTATE there is no relative form -- the value replaces the current
scale entirely.

@{b}MODEL HIDE@{ub} makes an instance invisible without removing it from
the scene. @{b}MODEL SHOW@{ub} makes it visible again. Hidden instances
are still part of the scene and are included in collision and
picking tests by default.

@{b}MODEL ERASE@{ub} removes the instance from the scene permanently.
The model bank it was built from is not affected.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Spinning cube with keyboard control
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 MODEL NEW m
40 FOR i=-1 TO 1 STEP 2
50   FOR j=-1 TO 1 STEP 2
60     FOR k=-1 TO 1 STEP 2
70       MODEL VERTEX m, i*0.5, j*0.5, k*0.5
80     NEXT k
90   NEXT j
100 NEXT i
110 REM  8 vertices now indexed 0-7; add 12 triangles for a cube
120 MODEL FACE m, 0,2,3 : MODEL FACE m, 0,3,1
130 MODEL FACE m, 4,5,7 : MODEL FACE m, 4,7,6
140 MODEL FACE m, 0,1,5 : MODEL FACE m, 0,5,4
150 MODEL FACE m, 2,6,7 : MODEL FACE m, 2,7,3
160 MODEL FACE m, 0,4,6 : MODEL FACE m, 0,6,2
170 MODEL FACE m, 1,3,7 : MODEL FACE m, 1,7,5
180 MODEL BUILD m
190 MODEL SHADING m FLAT
200 LIGHT TO 1,-1,-1
201 LIGHT AMBIENT 0.3
210 MODEL AT cube, m, 0,0,-4, 0,0,0
220 CAMERA 0,0,0, 0,0,0
230 DIM ry=0
240 DO
250   CLS
260   MODEL ROTATE cube, 1, ry, 0
270   LET ry = (ry+1) MOD 360
280   RENDER sc
290   WAIT SCREEN
300 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Place 50 random instances of the same model
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 MODEL NEW m
40 REM  ... build model geometry ...
50 MODEL BUILD m
60 DIM ids(50)
70 FOR i=0 TO 49
80   LET x=RND(40)-20
90   LET y=0
100  LET z=RND(40)-20
110  MODEL AT ids(i), m, x,y,z, 0,RND(360),0
120 NEXT i
130 CAMERA 0,5,20, -10,0,0
140 RENDER sc
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"MODEL NEW     " LINK MODEL_NEW}
@{"MODEL VERTEX  " LINK MODEL_VERTEX}
@{"MODEL SHADING " LINK MODEL_SHADING}
@{"MODEL ANIM    " LINK MODEL_ANIM}
@{"MODELx        " LINK MODEL_FUNCS}
@{"CAMERA        " LINK CAMERA}
@{"RENDER        " LINK RENDER}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  MODEL_SHADING -- Shading modes, colour, billboard
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE MODEL_SHADING "MODEL SHADING"
@{b}MODEL SHADING / MODEL INK / MODEL BILLBOARD@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}MODEL SHADING model FLAT@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL SHADING model SMOOTH@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL SHADING model WIRE [NOCULL] [SOLID]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL INK inst, colour@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL BILLBOARD inst@{fg text}
@{BODY}

@{b}MODEL SHADING@{ub} sets the rendering mode for a model bank. The chosen
mode applies to every instance of that model.

@{b}FLAT@{ub} renders each face as a single uniform colour, lit by the scene
light. The face colour comes from the @{b}INK@{ub} clause in @{b}MODEL FACE@{ub}, or
from the vertex colour of the first vertex if no INK was given.
Flat shading is the default.

@{b}SMOOTH@{ub} enables Gouraud shading. Each vertex is lit independently
and the colour is interpolated across the face. Per-vertex colours
set in @{b}MODEL VERTEX@{ub} are used as the base material colour.

@{b}WIRE@{ub} renders only the edges of the model as lines, with backfaces
culled by default (invisible from behind). @{b}NOCULL@{ub} draws edges
regardless of which way a face is pointing, useful for transparent
or open structures. @{b}SOLID@{ub} draws a solid filled (flat-shaded) back
face behind the wire edges, giving a hidden-surface wireframe.

All three shading modes support textured faces as specified in
@{b}MODEL FACE@{ub}. Textures override the flat or Gouraud colour on the
faces that carry them; the shading mode still applies to untextured
faces.

@{b}MODEL INK@{ub} sets a colour override on a specific @{b}instance@{ub}. When a
colour override is active every face of that instance is rendered
in that colour regardless of per-face or per-vertex colours. This
is useful for highlighting selected objects or tinting clones of
a shared model differently. Set @{b}colour@{ub} to -1 to remove the
override and restore the model's own colours.

@{b}MODEL BILLBOARD@{ub} enables billboard mode on an existing @{b}inst@{ub}. A
billboard instance always rotates to face the camera about the Y
axis. This is equivalent to the @{b}BILLBOARD@{ub} flag in @{b}MODEL AT@{ub} but
can be applied after placement. Billboard mode can only be turned
on, not off, for an existing instance; erase and re-place the
instance without the flag if needed.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Wireframe model with solid back faces and no backface cull
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 MODEL NEW m
40 REM  ... add vertices and faces ...
50 MODEL BUILD m
60 MODEL SHADING m WIRE NOCULL SOLID
70 MODEL AT i, m, 0,0,-5, 0,0,0
80 CAMERA 0,0,0, 0,0,0
90 RENDER sc
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Tint instances of the same model different colours
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 MODEL NEW m
40 REM  ... build model ...
50 MODEL BUILD m
60 MODEL SHADING m FLAT
70 MODEL AT r, m, -3,0,-8, 0,0,0
80 MODEL AT g, m,  0,0,-8, 0,0,0
90 MODEL AT b, m,  3,0,-8, 0,0,0
100 MODEL INK r, 2     : REM red
110 MODEL INK g, 4     : REM green
120 MODEL INK b, 1     : REM blue
130 CAMERA 0,0,0, 0,0,0
140 RENDER sc
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"MODEL VERTEX  " LINK MODEL_VERTEX}
@{"MODEL AT      " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"LIGHT TO      " LINK LIGHT}
@{"RENDER        " LINK RENDER}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  MODEL_ANIM -- Animation frames, parenting
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE MODEL_ANIM "MODEL ADDFRAME"
@{b}MODEL ADDFRAME / MODEL ANIM / MODEL PARENT / MODEL UNPARENT@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}MODEL ADDFRAME model, name$@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL ANIM PLAY inst, a$, b$, speed@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL ANIM STOP inst@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL ANIM FRAME inst, name$@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL PARENT inst, parent@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODEL UNPARENT inst@{fg text}
@{BODY}

@{b}Morph Animation@{ub}

SpecBAS animates 3D models using vertex morphing: each animation
frame stores an alternative set of vertex positions. The renderer
interpolates smoothly between two frames as animation plays, giving
the appearance of smooth deformation.

@{b}MODEL ADDFRAME model, name$@{ub} captures the model's current vertex
positions as a named animation frame. Build the model in one pose,
call @{b}MODEL ADDFRAME@{ub}, then change the vertex positions with
@{b}MODEL SETVERT@{ub}, call @{b}MODEL ADDFRAME@{ub} again with a different name,
and so on. The model does not need to be rebuilt between frame
captures.

@{b}MODEL ANIM PLAY inst, a$, b$, speed@{ub} starts animating an instance.
@{b}a$@{ub} and @{b}b$@{ub} are the names of the start and end frames. The
interpolation parameter advances by @{b}speed@{ub} per frame. When it
reaches 1.0 the animation loops back to 0.0 and continues. The
animation advances automatically on each @{b}RENDER@{ub} call; no loop code
is needed.

@{b}MODEL ANIM STOP inst@{ub} stops the animation at its current position.

@{b}MODEL ANIM FRAME inst, name$@{ub} jumps the animation to a specific
named frame immediately, halting any playing animation.

@{b}Parenting@{ub}

@{b}MODEL PARENT inst, parent@{ub} attaches @{b}inst@{ub} to @{b}parent@{ub} so that the
child inherits the parent's world transform. Moving or rotating the
parent automatically moves and rotates the child by the same amount.
The child's own position and rotation are relative to the parent.
Chains of any depth are supported (child of child of parent).

@{b}MODEL UNPARENT inst@{ub} detaches the instance from its parent. The
instance's position remains at the world position it had at the
moment of detachment.

@{b}PARENT@{ub} can also be set at placement time using the @{b}PARENT@{ub} clause
in @{b}MODEL AT@{ub}.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Morphing sphere: build two frames and animate
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 MODEL NEW m
40 DIM pi2=6.2832
50 REM  Frame A: sphere
60 FOR i=0 TO 31
70   LET a=i*pi2/32
80   MODEL VERTEX m, COS(a),SIN(a),0
90 NEXT i
100 MODEL ADDFRAME m, "sphere"
110 REM  Frame B: stretch sphere to oval
120 FOR i=0 TO 31
130   LET a=i*pi2/32
140   MODEL SETVERT m, i, COS(a)*2, SIN(a)*0.5, 0
150 NEXT i
160 MODEL ADDFRAME m, "oval"
170 REM  Add faces (ring) -- omitted for brevity
180 MODEL BUILD m
190 MODEL AT inst, m, 0,0,-5, 0,0,0
200 MODEL ANIM PLAY inst, "sphere","oval", 0.02
210 CAMERA 0,0,0, 0,0,0
220 DO
230   CLS : RENDER sc : WAIT SCREEN
240 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Solar system: planets orbit a sun
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 MODEL NEW msun  : REM  ... build sun mesh ...
40 MODEL NEW mplan : REM  ... build planet mesh ...
50 MODEL BUILD msun
60 MODEL BUILD mplan
70 MODEL AT sun, msun, 0,0,-10, 0,0,0
80 MODEL AT earth, mplan, 4,0,-10, 0,0,0 PARENT sun
90 MODEL AT moon, mplan, 6,0,-10, 0,0,0 PARENT earth
100 MODEL SCALE moon, 0.3
110 CAMERA 0,5,5, -30,0,0
120 DIM t=0
130 DO
140   CLS
150   MODEL ROTATE sun, 0, 1, 0
160   MODEL ROTATE earth, 0, 2, 0   : REM orbits with sun, spins too
170   MODEL ROTATE moon, 0, 5, 0    : REM orbits earth
180   RENDER sc
190   WAIT SCREEN
200 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"MODEL VERTEX  " LINK MODEL_VERTEX}
@{"MODEL AT      " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"MODELplaying  " LINK MODEL_INFO}
@{"MODELframe$   " LINK MODEL_INFO}
@{"MODELx        " LINK MODEL_FUNCS}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  CAMERA -- Camera control
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE CAMERA "CAMERA"
@{b}CAMERA / CAMERA MOVE / CAMERA ROTATE / CAMERA FACE@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}CAMERA x, y, z, rx, ry, rz [, fov]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}CAMERA MOVE dx, dy, dz@{fg text}
@{fg 1}CAMERA ROTATE drx, dry, drz@{fg text}
@{fg 1}CAMERA FACE x, y, z@{fg text}
@{fg 1}CAMERA FACE MODEL inst@{fg text}
@{BODY}

@{b}CAMERA@{ub} positions and orients the camera for the active scene.
@{b}x@{ub},@{b}y@{ub},@{b}z@{ub} is the camera's world position. @{b}rx@{ub},@{b}ry@{ub},@{b}rz@{ub} are the
Euler rotation angles in degrees: @{b}rx@{ub} tilts up/down (pitch), @{b}ry@{ub}
turns left/right (yaw), @{b}rz@{ub} rolls. The optional @{b}fov@{ub} sets the
horizontal field of view in degrees; values around 60 to 90 are
typical. If @{b}fov@{ub} is 0 or omitted the current FOV is preserved.

The camera is stored per-scene. When @{b}SCENE USE@{ub} switches to a
different scene its saved camera is restored automatically.

@{b}CAMERA MOVE@{ub} adds @{b}dx@{ub},@{b}dy@{ub},@{b}dz@{ub} to the camera's current world
position (relative move).

@{b}CAMERA ROTATE@{ub} adds @{b}drx@{ub},@{b}dry@{ub},@{b}drz@{ub} to the camera's current
rotation angles (relative rotate).

@{b}CAMERA FACE x, y, z@{ub} rotates the camera to look directly at the
world-space point @{b}x@{ub},@{b}y@{ub},@{b}z@{ub}. The camera position is not changed.
@{b}rx@{ub} and @{b}ry@{ub} are updated; @{b}rz@{ub} is set to zero (no roll).

@{b}CAMERA FACE MODEL inst@{ub} rotates the camera to look at the current
world position of instance @{b}inst@{ub}. Useful for tracking a moving
object. The camera position is not changed.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Orbit camera around the origin with mouse
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 REM  ... build model, place instances ...
40 DIM camr=0, camh=5, camd=15
50 DO
60   CLS
70   LET camr = camr + MOUSEdx*0.5
80   LET camh = CLAMP(camh - MOUSEdy*0.1, 1, 20)
90   LET cx = SIN(DEGTORAD(camr))*camd
100  LET cz = COS(DEGTORAD(camr))*camd
110  CAMERA cx, camh, cz, 0,0,0
120  CAMERA FACE 0,0,0
130  RENDER sc
140  WAIT SCREEN
150 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  First-person camera movement (WASD keys)
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 REM  ... build world geometry ...
40 DIM cx=0, cy=1, cz=0, ry=0
50 DO
60   CLS
70   IF INKEY(65)  THEN LET cx=cx-SIN(DEGTORAD(ry))*0.1 : LET cz=cz-COS(DEGTORAD(ry))*0.1
80   IF INKEY(68)  THEN LET cx=cx+SIN(DEGTORAD(ry))*0.1 : LET cz=cz+COS(DEGTORAD(ry))*0.1
90   IF INKEY(87)  THEN LET cx=cx+SIN(DEGTORAD(ry+90))*0.1 : LET cz=cz+COS(DEGTORAD(ry+90))*0.1
100  IF INKEY(83)  THEN LET cx=cx-SIN(DEGTORAD(ry+90))*0.1 : LET cz=cz-COS(DEGTORAD(ry+90))*0.1
110  LET ry = ry + MOUSEdx*0.3
120  CAMERA cx,cy,cz, 0,ry,0
130  RENDER sc
140  WAIT SCREEN
150 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"CAMERAx       " LINK CAMERA_FUNCS}
@{"SCENE         " LINK SCENE}
@{"RENDER        " LINK RENDER}
@{"SETNEAR       " LINK LIGHT}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  RENDER_3D -- RENDER command
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE RENDER "RENDER"
@{b}RENDER@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}RENDER scene [TO winID] [ASYNC [threads]]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}RENDER scene TO GRAPHIC id [ASYNC [threads]]@{fg text}
@{BODY}

Renders the scene identified by @{b}scene@{ub} into a window or graphic
bank. Without @{b}TO@{ub}, the current window is used.

@{b}TO winID@{ub} renders into a specific window. @{b}TO GRAPHIC id@{ub} renders
into a graphic bank rather than a window, which is useful for
generating textures or thumbnails at runtime.

@{b}ASYNC@{ub} enables multi-threaded rendering. The frame is divided into
horizontal bands, each rendered by a separate thread in parallel.
Without a thread count, one extra thread is used. Specify @{b}threads@{ub}
to control how many CPU threads to employ; sensible values are 2
to the number of logical processors on the machine. Rendering a
complex scene with @{b}ASYNC 4@{ub} on a quad-core machine can be two to
three times faster than single-threaded rendering.

If the target window uses 32 bits per pixel (created with
@{b}WINDOW NEW ... 32@{ub}) the 32bpp renderer is used automatically,
giving higher colour quality. Otherwise the 8bpp palette renderer
is used, which is faster on software targets.

@{b}RENDER@{ub} does not clear the window first. Call @{b}CLS@{ub} before @{b}RENDER@{ub}
to erase the previous frame, or leave it out to let the 3D scene
draw over whatever was previously on screen (useful for overlaying
HUD elements).

The call blocks until rendering is complete even when @{b}ASYNC@{ub} is
used, so the window is ready to display as soon as @{b}RENDER@{ub} returns.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Basic render loop at screen refresh rate
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 REM  ... build and place models ...
40 DO
50   CLS
60   RENDER sc
70   WAIT SCREEN
80 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Multi-threaded render into a 32bpp window
20 WINDOW NEW w, 0,0, SCRw,SCRh, 32
30 WINDOW w
40 SCENE NEW sc
50 REM  ... build and place models ...
60 DO
70   WINDOW w : CLS
80   RENDER sc TO w ASYNC 4
90   WAIT SCREEN
100 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"SCENE         " LINK SCENE}
@{"CAMERA        " LINK CAMERA}
@{"LIGHT TO      " LINK LIGHT}
@{"MODEL AT      " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"WINDOW        " LINK WINDOW}
@{"GRAPHIC       " LINK GRAPHIC}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE

; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  MODEL_FUNCS -- Instance position/rotation/scale readback
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE MODEL_FUNCS "MODELx"
@{b}MODELx / MODELy / MODELz / MODELrx / MODELry / MODELrz / MODELscale@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}MODELx(inst)@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODELy(inst)@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODELz(inst)@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODELrx(inst)@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODELry(inst)@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODELrz(inst)@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODELscale(inst)@{fg text}
@{BODY}

These functions return the current world-space position, rotation
and scale of an instance. @{b}inst@{ub} is the instance ID returned by a
previous @{b}MODEL AT@{ub} call.

@{b}MODELx@{ub}, @{b}MODELy@{ub} and @{b}MODELz@{ub} return the X, Y and Z world position.
@{b}MODELrx@{ub}, @{b}MODELry@{ub} and @{b}MODELrz@{ub} return the X, Y and Z rotation
in degrees. @{b}MODELscale@{ub} returns the uniform scale factor.

All values reflect the instance's own transform, not the result
of any parent transform. If you need the absolute world position
of a child instance, you must compute the parent transform yourself.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Keep a second object 2 units above the first
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 REM  ... build models leader and follower ...
40 MODEL AT lead, m, 0,0,-5, 0,0,0
50 MODEL AT follow, m, 0,2,-5, 0,0,0
60 DO
70   CLS
80   MODEL ROTATE lead, 0,1,0
90   REM  Follow leader's XZ position, stay 2 units above it
100  MODEL MOVE TO follow, MODELx(lead), MODELy(lead)+2, MODELz(lead)
110  MODEL ROTATE TO follow, MODELrx(lead), MODELry(lead), MODELrz(lead)
120  RENDER sc
130  WAIT SCREEN
140 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Display instance coordinates as HUD text
20 RENDER sc
30 PRINT AT 0,0; "X=";MODELx(player);" Y=";MODELy(player);" Z=";MODELz(player)
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"MODEL AT      " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"MODELplaying  " LINK MODEL_INFO}
@{"CAMERAx       " LINK CAMERA_FUNCS}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  MODEL_INFO -- Playing state, collision, picking
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE MODEL_INFO "MODELplaying"
@{b}MODELplaying / MODELframe$ / MODELcoll / POINT3D / FACEat@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}MODELplaying(inst)@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODELframe$(inst)@{fg text}
@{fg 1}MODELcoll(instA, instB)@{fg text}
@{fg 1}POINT3D(sx, sy)@{fg text}
@{fg 1}FACEat(inst, sx, sy)@{fg text}
@{BODY}

@{b}MODELplaying(inst)@{ub} returns 1 if the instance currently has an
animation playing (started by @{b}MODEL ANIM PLAY@{ub}), or 0 if not.

@{b}MODELframe$(inst)@{ub} returns the name of the animation frame the
instance is currently closest to. This is the name of whichever of
the two frames (@{b}a$@{ub} or @{b}b$@{ub} in @{b}MODEL ANIM PLAY@{ub}) the blend parameter
is nearest to. Useful for triggering events at specific points in
an animation cycle.

@{b}MODELcoll(instA, instB)@{ub} returns 1 if the bounding spheres of the
two instances overlap, or 0 if they do not. The bounding sphere is
computed by @{b}MODEL BUILD@{ub} to enclose all vertices of the model,
scaled by the instance's current scale factor. This is a fast
approximate collision test; for exact polygon-level collision you
should follow up with more precise checks on your own geometry.

@{b}POINT3D(sx, sy)@{ub} casts a ray from the camera through screen pixel
(@{b}sx@{ub},@{b}sy@{ub}) and returns the instance ID of the closest instance hit
by that ray, or -1 if no instance is intersected. This is the
primary picking function for mouse-driven 3D interaction. The test
is against each instance's bounding sphere.

@{b}FACEat(inst, sx, sy)@{ub} tests a ray through screen pixel (@{b}sx@{ub},@{b}sy@{ub})
against the actual triangles of @{b}inst@{ub} and returns the zero-based
index of the hit face, or -1 if no face is hit. This provides an
exact face-level pick after @{b}POINT3D@{ub} has identified the instance.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Click to select a model instance
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 REM  ... place several instances ...
40 DIM selected = -1
50 DO
60   CLS
70   IF MOUSEBTN(1) THEN
80     LET selected = POINT3D(MOUSEx, MOUSEy)
90   END IF
100  IF selected >= 0 THEN
110    MODEL INK selected, 6   : REM highlight yellow
120  END IF
130  RENDER sc
140  WAIT SCREEN
150 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Collision response between two moving spheres
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 MODEL NEW ball
40 REM  ... build ball mesh, build it ...
50 MODEL AT a, ball, -3,0,-8, 0,0,0
60 MODEL AT b, ball,  3,0,-8, 0,0,0
70 CAMERA 0,2,0, -5,0,0
80 DIM va=0.05, vb=-0.05
90 DO
100  LET xa=MODELx(a)+va
110  IF ABS(xa) > 4 OR MODELcoll(a,b) THEN LET va=-va : LET xa=MODELx(a)+va
120  LET xb=MODELx(b)+vb
130  IF ABS(xb) > 4 OR MODELcoll(a,b) THEN LET vb=-vb : LET xb=MODELx(b)+vb
140  MODEL MOVE TO a, xa, 0, -8
150  MODEL MOVE TO b, xb, 0, -8
160  CLS : RENDER sc : WAIT SCREEN
170 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Show which face of a model was clicked
20 DO
30   CLS
40   RENDER sc
50   IF MOUSEBTN(1) THEN
60     LET hit = POINT3D(MOUSEx, MOUSEy)
70     IF hit >= 0 THEN
80       LET f = FACEat(hit, MOUSEx, MOUSEy)
90       PRINT AT 0,0; "Face "; f
100    END IF
110  END IF
120  WAIT SCREEN
130 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"MODEL ANIM    " LINK MODEL_ANIM}
@{"MODEL AT      " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"MODELx        " LINK MODEL_FUNCS}
@{"MOUSEBTN      " LINK MOUSEBTN}
@{"MOUSEx        " LINK MOUSEx}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  CAMERA_FUNCS -- Camera position/rotation readback
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE CAMERA_FUNCS "CAMERAx"
@{b}CAMERAx / CAMERAy / CAMERAz / CAMERArx / CAMERAry / CAMERArz / CAMERAfov@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}CAMERAx@{fg text}
@{fg 1}CAMERAy@{fg text}
@{fg 1}CAMERAz@{fg text}
@{fg 1}CAMERArx@{fg text}
@{fg 1}CAMERAry@{fg text}
@{fg 1}CAMERArz@{fg text}
@{fg 1}CAMERAfov@{fg text}
@{BODY}

These functions return the current state of the active scene's
camera. They take no arguments.

@{b}CAMERAx@{ub}, @{b}CAMERAy@{ub} and @{b}CAMERAz@{ub} return the world position.
@{b}CAMERArx@{ub}, @{b}CAMERAry@{ub} and @{b}CAMERArz@{ub} return the Euler rotation
angles in degrees. @{b}CAMERAfov@{ub} returns the horizontal field of
view in degrees.

These are useful when the camera is modified by @{b}CAMERA MOVE@{ub},
@{b}CAMERA ROTATE@{ub} or @{b}CAMERA FACE@{ub} over many frames and you need to
read back the accumulated state, for example to save a viewpoint
or to calculate where the camera is pointing.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Print camera position every frame
20 DO
30   CLS
40   CAMERA MOVE 0, 0, -0.1      : REM  dolly forward
50   RENDER sc
60   PRINT AT 0,0; "Cam Z="; CAMERAz
70   WAIT SCREEN
80 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Save and restore a camera viewpoint
20 LET sx=CAMERAx : LET sy=CAMERAy : LET sz=CAMERAz
30 LET srx=CAMERArx : LET sry=CAMERAry : LET srz=CAMERArz
40 LET sfov=CAMERAfov
50 REM  ... move camera around ...
60 REM  Restore:
70 CAMERA sx,sy,sz, srx,sry,srz, sfov
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"CAMERA        " LINK CAMERA}
@{"MODELx        " LINK MODEL_FUNCS}
@{"SCENE         " LINK SCENE}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE

@NODE RAINBOW "RAINBOW"
@{b}RAINBOW@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}RAINBOW idx1 TO idx2@{fg text}
@{fg 1}RAINBOW HSV idx1 TO idx2@{fg text}
@{BODY}

Fills a contiguous range of palette entries with a smooth colour
gradient. The colour at @{b}idx1@{ub} and the colour at @{b}idx2@{ub} are read from
the current palette and left unchanged; all entries between them
are replaced with evenly-spaced intermediate colours.

Both @{b}idx1@{ub} and @{b}idx2@{ub} are palette indices in the range 0 to 255.
They may be given in either order; RAINBOW always works from the
lower index to the higher.

The plain @{b}RAINBOW@{ub} form interpolates linearly in RGB space. This
is simple and predictable but can produce muddy intermediate colours
when the start and end colours are far apart in hue (for example
a gradient from blue to yellow passes through a dull grey).

@{b}RAINBOW HSV@{ub} interpolates in HSV (hue-saturation-value) space
instead. This routes the gradient around the colour wheel rather
than cutting across it, giving vivid, saturated intermediate colours
for most start/end pairs. For a gradient between two complementary
colours such as red and cyan, HSV interpolation follows the short
arc of the colour wheel; you can reverse the direction by setting
up the endpoint colours yourself before calling RAINBOW.

Neither form alters the colours at @{b}idx1@{ub} or @{b}idx2@{ub} themselves.
If @{b}idx1@{ub} equals @{b}idx2@{ub}, no entries are changed.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Fire palette: black at 0, dark red at 64, orange at
20 REM  128, yellow at 192, white at 255
30 PALETTE 0, 0,0,0
40 PALETTE 64, 128,0,0
50 PALETTE 128, 255,64,0
60 PALETTE 192, 255,200,0
70 PALETTE 255, 255,255,255
80 RAINBOW 0 TO 64
90 RAINBOW 64 TO 128
100 RAINBOW 128 TO 192
110 RAINBOW 192 TO 255
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Spectrum palette using HSV interpolation
20 REM  Set red at 16, green at 112, blue at 208
30 PALETTE 16, 255,0,0
40 PALETTE 112, 0,255,0
50 PALETTE 208, 0,0,255
60 RAINBOW HSV 16 TO 112
70 RAINBOW HSV 112 TO 208
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Animated palette cycling on a rainbow band
20 PALETTE 0, 255,0,0
30 PALETTE 32, 255,165,0
40 PALETTE 64, 255,255,0
50 PALETTE 96, 0,200,0
60 PALETTE 128, 0,100,255
70 PALETTE 159, 140,0,200
80 RAINBOW HSV 0 TO 32
90 RAINBOW HSV 32 TO 64
100 RAINBOW HSV 64 TO 96
110 RAINBOW HSV 96 TO 128
120 RAINBOW HSV 128 TO 159
130 REM  Cycle the palette each frame
140 DO
150   PALETTE SHIFT 0, 159, 1
160   WAIT SCREEN
170 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"PALETTE       " LINK PALETTE}
@{"HSV           " LINK HSV}
@{"RGB           " LINK RGB}
@{"RGBTOHSV      " LINK RGBTOHSV}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE

@NODE ORIGIN "ORIGIN"
@{b}ORIGIN@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}ORIGIN x1,y1 TO x2,y2 [FLIP]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}ORIGIN x1,y1, w,h [FLIP]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}ORIGIN x1,y1 [FLIP]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}ORIGIN FLIP@{fg text}
@{fg 1}ORIGIN OFF@{fg text}
@{BODY}

@{b}ORIGIN@{ub} installs a coordinate mapping on the current window.
After an ORIGIN call, all drawing commands use the logical coordinate
system you define rather than raw pixel coordinates. This lets you
write programs in any coordinate system -- mathematical, game-world,
or data-space -- without manually scaling every value.

@{b}ORIGIN x1,y1 TO x2,y2@{ub} maps logical point (@{b}x1@{ub},@{b}y1@{ub}) to the top-left
of the window and (@{b}x2@{ub},@{b}y2@{ub}) to the bottom-right. All drawing
coordinates are scaled linearly between these extremes.

@{b}ORIGIN x1,y1, w,h@{ub} is equivalent but uses a width and height
instead of a second corner point.

@{b}ORIGIN x1,y1@{ub} (without TO or dimensions) sets only the origin
point -- the logical position that maps to the window's top-left
corner -- without changing the scale. Coordinates still advance
one unit per pixel.

Any of the above forms may end with @{b}FLIP@{ub} to invert the Y axis
so that Y increases upward (mathematical convention) rather than
downward (screen convention).

@{b}ORIGIN FLIP@{ub} alone toggles the Y-axis inversion without changing
the current origin or scale.

@{b}ORIGIN OFF@{ub} removes any active coordinate mapping and returns to
raw pixel coordinates.

The functions @{b}ORGx@{ub} and @{b}ORGy@{ub} return the current origin offset
in pixels, and @{b}LTOPx@{ub}/@{b}LTOPy@{ub} and @{b}PTOLx@{ub}/@{b}PTOLy@{ub} convert between
logical and pixel coordinates.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Plot a sine wave with Y increasing upward,
20 REM  x from 0 to 2*PI, y from -1.5 to 1.5
30 ORIGIN 0,-1.5 TO TAU,1.5 FLIP
40 INK 2 : PLOT 0,0
50 FOR x=0 TO TAU STEP TAU/200
60   DRAW x, SIN(x)
70 NEXT x
80 ORIGIN OFF
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Data chart: map data range 0..100 onto window
20 WINDOW NEW w, 50,50, 400,300
30 WINDOW w
40 ORIGIN 0,0 TO 100,100 FLIP
50 INK 1 : DRAW 0,0 TO 100,0   : REM x-axis
60 INK 1 : DRAW 0,0 TO 0,100   : REM y-axis
70 INK 2
80 FOR i=0 TO 99
90   PLOT i, RND(100)
100 NEXT i
110 ORIGIN OFF
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"ORGx          " LINK ORGX}
@{"ORGy          " LINK ORGX}
@{"LTOPx         " LINK LTOPX}
@{"PTOLx         " LINK LTOPX}
@{"WINDOW ORIGIN " LINK WINDOW}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  PARTICLE / FORCE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE PARTICLE "PARTICLE"
@{b}PARTICLE / FORCE@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}PARTICLE array() [FRICTION f] [GRAVITY magnitude, heading]@{fg text}
@{fg 1}FORCE array(), magnitude, heading@{fg text}
@{BODY}

SpecBAS provides a simple 2D particle physics system driven by
a numeric array. Each row in the array represents one particle,
and must have at least 7 columns in this order:

@{CODE}
  Column 0: x         world X position
  Column 1: y         world Y position
  Column 2: colour    palette index for display
  Column 3: radius    collision radius in pixels
  Column 4: mass      particle mass (heavier = harder to deflect)
  Column 5: dx        X velocity (pixels per step)
  Column 6: dy        Y velocity (pixels per step)
@{BODY}

Any additional columns are ignored. All values are floating-point.

@{b}PARTICLE@{ub}

@{b}PARTICLE array()@{ub} advances all particles by one physics step:

Velocities (@{b}dx@{ub}, @{b}dy@{ub}) are added to positions (@{b}x@{ub}, @{b}y@{ub}). Every pair
of particles whose circles overlap undergoes an elastic collision:
velocities are exchanged weighted by mass, so heavy particles
deflect light ones more than the reverse. @{b}GRAVITY magnitude, heading@{ub}
adds a gravitational acceleration in the given direction to every
particle's velocity before the step. @{b}FRICTION f@{ub} multiplies every
velocity by @{b}f@{ub} after each step; values just below 1.0 (e.g. 0.99)
give a gentle damping effect; 1.0 means no friction.

The @{b}heading@{ub} follows the same angle convention as DRAW and TURNS
(degrees or radians according to the current DEGREES/RADIANS setting);
0 degrees points right, 90 degrees points down (unless ORIGIN FLIP
is active).

@{b}PARTICLE@{ub} modifies the array in place. It does not draw anything;
use @{b}MULTIPLOT array()@{ub} or your own loop with @{b}CIRCLE@{ub} to render the
particles each frame.

@{b}FORCE@{ub}

@{b}FORCE array(), magnitude, heading@{ub} applies a single gravitational
impulse to all particles (dx += gx, dy += gy) without doing collision
detection. Use this when you want to handle collisions yourself or
need a very fast update without interaction physics.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Simple particle fountain
20 DIM p(50, 7)
30 FOR i=0 TO 49
40   p(i,0) = SCRw/2           : REM  x - screen centre
50   p(i,1) = SCRh/2           : REM  y
60   p(i,2) = 2 + (i MOD 6)    : REM  colour
70   p(i,3) = 3                 : REM  radius
80   p(i,4) = 1                 : REM  mass
90   p(i,5) = (RND(6)-3)*2     : REM  dx
100  p(i,6) = -(RND(4)+2)*2    : REM  dy (upward)
110 NEXT i
120 DO
130  CLS
140  PARTICLE p() FRICTION 0.995 GRAVITY 1, 90
150  FOR i=0 TO 49
160    IF p(i,1) > SCRh+5 THEN
170      p(i,1) = SCRh/2 : p(i,5)=(RND(6)-3)*2 : p(i,6)=-(RND(4)+2)*2
180    END IF
190  NEXT i
200  INK -1
210  FOR i=0 TO 49
220    CIRCLE p(i,0),p(i,1),p(i,3)
230  NEXT i
240  WAIT SCREEN
250 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  FORCE example: particles drift in wind
20 DIM p(20, 7)
30 FOR i=0 TO 19
40   p(i,0) = RND(SCRw) : p(i,1) = RND(SCRh)
50   p(i,2) = 6 : p(i,3) = 4 : p(i,4) = 1
60   p(i,5) = 0 : p(i,6) = 0
70 NEXT i
80 DO
90   CLS
100  FORCE p(), 0.2, 0      : REM  gentle rightward breeze
110  FOR i=0 TO 19
120    IF p(i,0) > SCRw THEN p(i,0) = 0
130  NEXT i
140  FOR i=0 TO 19
150    CIRCLE p(i,0),p(i,1),4
160  NEXT i
170  WAIT SCREEN
180 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"PLOT          " LINK PLOT}
@{"CIRCLE        " LINK CIRCLE}
@{"TURNS         " LINK TURNS}
@{"DEGREES       " LINK DEGREES}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  TRANSFORM3D / PROJECT3D
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE TRANSFORM3D "TRANSFORM3D"
@{b}TRANSFORM3D / PROJECT3D@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}TRANSFORM3D src() [TO dst()] {MOVE dx,dy,dz | ROTATE rx,ry,rz | SCALE sx,sy,sz} ...@{fg text}
@{fg 1}PROJECT3D src() TO dst() [MOVE tx,ty,tz] [ROTATE rx,ry,rz] [SCALE sx,sy,sz]@{fg text}
@{BODY}

These commands operate on a 2D numeric array of 3D points. The array
must have at least 3 columns per row, representing the X, Y and Z
coordinate of each point. Any extra columns are carried through
unchanged.

@{b}TRANSFORM3D@{ub}

@{b}TRANSFORM3D src()@{ub} applies one or more geometric transforms to every
point in @{b}src()@{ub}. Transforms are applied in the order written. Without
the @{b}TO dst()@{ub} clause the source array is modified in place; with it
the results go to @{b}dst()@{ub} and @{b}src()@{ub} is left unchanged.

@{b}MOVE dx,dy,dz@{ub} translates every point by the given offset.

@{b}ROTATE rx,ry,rz@{ub} rotates every point about the origin by @{b}rx@{ub} degrees
around the X axis (pitch), @{b}ry@{ub} degrees around Y (yaw), and @{b}rz@{ub}
degrees around Z (roll). Rotation order is X then Y then Z.

@{b}SCALE sx,sy,sz@{ub} multiplies every point's coordinates by the given
scale factors. Use equal values for uniform scaling.

Multiple clauses may be chained on one line:

@{CODE}
TRANSFORM3D pts() SCALE 2,2,2 ROTATE 0,45,0 MOVE 10,0,-5
@{BODY}

This scales, then rotates, then translates -- in that order.

@{b}PROJECT3D@{ub}

@{b}PROJECT3D src() TO dst()@{ub} projects each 3D point in @{b}src()@{ub} through
the current scene camera (FOV and near plane as set by @{b}CAMERA@{ub} and
@{b}SETNEAR@{ub}) and writes the resulting 2D screen coordinates to @{b}dst()@{ub}.
The optional @{b}MOVE@{ub}, @{b}ROTATE@{ub} and @{b}SCALE@{ub} clauses apply a world transform
to the source points before projection, matching the transform
applied to model instances by the 3D engine. Each row of @{b}dst()@{ub}
receives the projected X and Y screen coordinates; the Z value
(depth) is written to the third column if @{b}dst()@{ub} has one.

Points that fall outside the view frustum still produce values in
@{b}dst()@{ub} -- check depth against @{b}SETNEAR@{ub} or clip manually if needed.

These commands are independent of the 3D scene system. They work
on raw arrays and do not require a scene bank or any model banks.
They are complementary to the scene-based commands described in
@{"3D Graphics" LINK Sec_3D}.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Transform and project a star field manually
20 DIM stars(100, 3)
30 FOR i=0 TO 99
40   stars(i,0) = RND(200)-100
50   stars(i,1) = RND(200)-100
60   stars(i,2) = RND(200)-100
70 NEXT i
80 DIM screen(100, 2)
90 CAMERA 0,0,0, 0,0,0, 70
100 DO
110  CLS
120  TRANSFORM3D stars() ROTATE 0,0.5,0.2
130  PROJECT3D stars() TO screen()
140  FOR i=0 TO 99
150    IF screen(i,2) > 0 THEN
160      PLOT screen(i,0), screen(i,1)
170    END IF
180  NEXT i
190  WAIT SCREEN
200 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Label a 3D model's vertices with screen-space text
20 REM  Assumes scene sc and instance inst are already set up
30 DIM verts(8, 3)
40 REM  ... fill verts() with the cube's world-space corners ...
50 DIM screen(8, 3)
60 PROJECT3D verts() TO screen() MOVE MODELx(inst),MODELy(inst),MODELz(inst)
70 FOR i=0 TO 7
80   IF screen(i,2) > 0 THEN
90     PRINT AT screen(i,1), screen(i,0); i
100  END IF
110 NEXT i
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"3D Graphics   " LINK Sec_3D}
@{"CAMERA        " LINK CAMERA_3D}
@{"SETNEAR       " LINK LIGHT}
@{"MODEL AT      " LINK MODEL_AT}
@{"MODELx        " LINK MODEL_FUNCS}
@{"TRANSFORM2D   " LINK TRANSFORM2D}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE

; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  LIGHT -- LIGHT TO, LIGHT AMBIENT, LIGHT FOG, SETNEAR
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE LIGHT "LIGHT"
@{b}LIGHT TO / LIGHT AMBIENT / LIGHT COLOUR / LIGHT FOG / SETNEAR@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}LIGHT TO dx, dy, dz@{fg text}
@{fg 1}LIGHT AMBIENT level@{fg text}
@{fg 1}LIGHT COLOUR $RRGGBB@{fg text}
@{fg 1}LIGHT COLOUR red, green, blue@{fg text}
@{fg 1}LIGHT FOG near, far, colour@{fg text}
@{fg 1}LIGHT FOG OFF@{fg text}
@{fg 1}SETNEAR dist@{fg text}
@{BODY}

@{b}Directional Light@{ub}

@{b}LIGHT TO dx, dy, dz@{ub} sets the direction of the scene's single
directional light. The vector (@{b}dx@{ub},@{b}dy@{ub},@{b}dz@{ub}) points from the light
source toward the scene. It does not need to be normalised. The
default is (0,-1,0) -- directly overhead. Light affects all
shading modes except wireframe.

@{b}LIGHT AMBIENT level@{ub} sets the ambient light intensity as a value
from 0.0 (total darkness) to 1.0 (fully lit regardless of face
angle). The default is 0.25. Ambient light prevents faces pointing
away from the light from going completely black.

@{b}Light Colour@{ub}

@{b}LIGHT COLOUR@{ub} sets the colour of the directional light. By default
the light is white, which leaves surface colours unchanged. A
coloured light tints all lit surfaces toward that colour -- a red
light makes surfaces appear redder, a blue light makes them appear
bluer.

The single-argument form takes an @{b}$RRGGBB@{ub} hex colour value, which
is the same format used by @{b}RGB()@{ub} and the rest of SpecBAS.

The three-argument form takes separate @{b}red@{ub}, @{b}green@{ub} and @{b}blue@{ub}
components each in the range 0 to 255. Both forms are equivalent.

Note that light colour interacts with surface colour multiplicatively,
as it does in the real world. A surface only reflects light of its
own colour: a pure red surface under a pure blue light appears
black, because red paint reflects no blue light. To tint all
surfaces equally, use a near-white colour with a slight hue rather
than a fully saturated primary. To restore white light, use
@{b}LIGHT COLOUR 255, 255, 255@{ub} or @{b}LIGHT COLOUR $FFFFFF@{ub}.

In 8bpp mode the light colour is baked into the shade table when
it is next rebuilt, so a palette change or any call to
@{b}LIGHT COLOUR@{ub} will trigger a shade table rebuild on the next
@{b}RENDER@{ub}. In 32bpp mode the colour is applied per-pixel at no extra
cost.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Scene with a warm low-sun lighting and fog
20 SCENE NEW sc
30 REM  ... build models and scene ...
40 LIGHT TO 0.5,-0.3,1      : REM  sun from side and behind
50 LIGHT AMBIENT 0.2
60 LIGHT COLOUR 255,220,160 : REM  warm golden sunlight
70 LIGHT FOG 15, 40, 7      : REM  white mist from 15 to 40 units
80 CAMERA 0,1,0, 0,0,0
90 DO
100  CLS
110  RENDER sc
120  WAIT SCREEN
130 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Night scene with moonlight (cool blue-white)
20 LIGHT TO 0,-1,0
30 LIGHT AMBIENT 0.05
40 LIGHT COLOUR 180,200,255 : REM  cool blue-white moonlight
50 LIGHT FOG 5, 15, 0       : REM  black fog, 5 to 15 units
60 SETNEAR 0.05             : REM  allow close-up torch view
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Cycle light colour through red, green, blue
20 DIM t=0
30 DO
40   LET r = INT((SIN(t)        * 0.5 + 0.5) * 255)
50   LET g = INT((SIN(t + 2.09) * 0.5 + 0.5) * 255)
60   LET b = INT((SIN(t + 4.19) * 0.5 + 0.5) * 255)
70   LIGHT COLOUR r, g, b
80   LET t = t + 0.02
90   CLS : RENDER sc : WAIT SCREEN
100 LOOP
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"MODEL SHADING " LINK MODEL_SHADING}
@{"CAMERA        " LINK CAMERA_3D}
@{"RENDER        " LINK RENDER_3D}
@{"RGB           " LINK RGB}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE

; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  COMPILE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE COMPILE "COMPILE"
@{b}COMPILE@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}COMPILE filename$ [, caption$] [LINE startline] [BANK id [,id ...]]@{fg text}
@{BODY}

Creates a standalone executable from the current program and writes
it to @{b}filename$@{ub}. The output file is a copy of the SpecBAS interpreter
with the current program embedded as a payload. Running the output
file launches directly into the program without the editor.

COMPILE may only be used from the primary thread; it will error if
called from a secondary thread.

@{b}caption$@{ub} sets the title bar text of the compiled program's window.
If omitted the window has no caption.

@{b}LINE startline@{ub} causes the compiled program to begin execution at
the given line number rather than from the first line. This is
useful when the program contains setup or menu code at the top that
should be skipped when running standalone.

@{b}BANK id@{ub} embeds one or more bank files into the executable. Multiple
bank IDs are listed separated by commas. The embedded banks are
available to the compiled program at startup, exactly as if they
had been loaded with LOAD BANK before the program runs. This allows
a compiled program to be distributed as a single file even when it
depends on graphic, sound or model banks.

The output file has the same extension and format as the SpecBAS
interpreter itself on the current platform. On most systems you
will need to ensure the output file has execute permission set
before running it.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Save a self-contained game executable with its
20 REM  graphic and sound banks embedded
30 GRAPHIC NEW gfx : REM  ... load graphics ...
40 SAMPLE NEW sfx  : REM  ... load samples ...
50 COMPILE "mygame.exe", "My Game" BANK gfx, sfx
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Compile starting at line 1000 (skip the menu system)
20 COMPILE "game_direct.exe", "My Game" LINE 1000
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"SAVE          " LINK SAVE}
@{"LOAD          " LINK LOAD}
@{"BANK          " LINK BANK}
@{"EXECUTE       " LINK EXECUTE}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  LLIST
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE LLIST "LLIST"
@{b}LLIST@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}LLIST filename$ [, width [, height]]@{fg text}
@{BODY}

Renders the current program listing as a pixel-art image in the
style of a magazine type-in listing and saves it to @{b}filename$@{ub}.
The listing is drawn using the system font onto a newly created
graphic bank, which is then saved as an image file.

@{b}width@{ub} sets the column width of the listing in characters. If
omitted or set to -1 it defaults to the current display width in
characters. Long lines are word-wrapped to fit the column width.

@{b}height@{ub} sets the maximum number of lines per column before a new
column is started. If omitted or set to -1 the listing occupies a
single column as tall as needed. When a @{b}height@{ub} is given, the
output is laid out in multiple columns side by side -- useful for
producing a compact magazine-page style image.

The image is saved in whatever format is determined by @{b}filename$@{ub}'s
extension. The graphic bank used for rendering is deleted
automatically after saving; it does not remain in memory.

If the program was loaded using @{b}INCLUDE@{ub}, only the included portion
of the program is listed.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Save the listing as a single-column image
20 LLIST "listing.png"
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Magazine layout: 40 chars wide, 60 lines per column
20 LLIST "magazine.png", 40, 60
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"LIST          " LINK LIST}
@{"SAVE          " LINK SAVE}
@{"GRAPHIC       " LINK GRAPHIC}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  KEYBOARD
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE KEYBOARD "KEYBOARD"
@{b}KEYBOARD@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}KEYBOARD name$@{fg text}
@{BODY}

Loads a keyboard layout definition file from @{b}name$@{ub}. This replaces
the current keyboard mapping, allowing programs to support
non-English keyboards or custom input schemes at runtime.

A keyboard definition file maps physical key codes to the characters
they produce, both with and without modifier keys. The format is
determined by the SpecBAS keyboard definition system; refer to the
keyboard definition files supplied with SpecBAS for the correct
layout.

After @{b}KEYBOARD@{ub} the new mapping takes effect immediately for all
subsequent @{b}INKEY$@{ub}, @{b}INPUT@{ub} and @{b}KEY@{ub} operations.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Load a French keyboard layout
20 KEYBOARD "keyboards/fr.kbd"
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"INKEY$        " LINK INKEY$}
@{"INPUT         " LINK INPUT}
@{"KEY           " LINK KEY}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  HELP
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE HELP_CMD "HELP"
@{b}HELP@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}HELP@{fg text}
@{fg 1}HELP keyword$@{fg text}
@{BODY}

Opens the SpecBAS online help viewer from within a running program.

@{b}HELP@{ub} without an argument opens the help at the contents page.

@{b}HELP keyword$@{ub} jumps directly to the entry for @{b}keyword$@{ub}, exactly
as if the user had pressed F1 with that keyword under the cursor in
the editor. If no entry exists for the given keyword the contents
page is shown instead.

@{b}HELP@{ub} is most useful during development for quick reference, but
can also be used to provide in-program documentation or guidance
to the user in an interactive tutorial.

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Open help for the CIRCLE command
20 HELP "CIRCLE"
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{CODE}
@{BASIC}
10 REM  Context-sensitive help using the user's input
20 INPUT "Enter a keyword: "; kw$
30 HELP kw$
@{ENDBASIC}
@{BODY}

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"  Contents    " LINK Main}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE


; ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  INSTALL
; ----------------------------------------------------------------

@NODE INSTALL "INSTALL"
@{b}INSTALL@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{fg 1}INSTALL@{fg text}
@{BODY}

On macOS, @{b}INSTALL@{ub} copies the SpecBAS support files from inside the
application bundle to the user's home directory (@{b}~/specbas@{ub}), making
them accessible for programs that expect to find resources at the
default SpecBAS paths. It also resets the palette to the default
and clears the screen.

On all other platforms @{b}INSTALL@{ub} does nothing and returns without
error.

@{b}INSTALL@{ub} is typically only needed once, on first run after installing
SpecBAS from a macOS disk image. It is safe to call it from a
program; if the files are already in place they will be overwritten
with fresh copies from the bundle.

@{b}See Also@{ub}

@{CODE}
@{"COMPILE       " LINK COMPILE}
@{"SAVE          " LINK SAVE}
@{BODY}

@ENDNODE
